    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>New package skillspector (security scanner for AI agent skills) + -mcp subpackage. Resubmit of the declined SR 1362984: source now comes from a reproducible obs_scm git snapshot of upstream main (2.3.9~git20260701.326a2b48, commit 326a2b48) because NVIDIA/skillspector publishes no git tags, no GitHub releases and nothing on PyPI. source_validator passes. Final piece of the langchain/langgraph cone: requires langchain-core, langgraph, langsmith&gt;=0.7.30 etc. Please co-stage with the sibling devel:languages:python cone SRs by pluskalm (Staging:adi:38) and python-langsmith (SR 1362932, science:machinelearning-&gt;Factory) or the build is unresolvable. Builds clean (noarch, 0 rpmlint badness, %check 1249 passed) where the stack is available.</comment>
    <requestid>1363013</requestid>
  </revision>
  <revision rev="2" vrev="2">
    <srcmd5>52dc3c93cc9c11f9a6b32330338e8fcb</srcmd5>
    <version>2.3.9~git20260701.326a2b48</version>
    <time>1783087859</time>
    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>Relax three over-strict runtime dependency floors that upstream set at initial release as uv-lock pin-inflation (never touched in 18 later commits), not real API requirements: pydantic &gt;=2.12.0-&gt;2.11.7, rich &gt;=14.3.0-&gt;14.0.0, typer &gt;=0.23.0-&gt;0.16.0. The code uses only long-stable APIs of each; verified against Leap 16.0's shipped versions (pydantic 2.11.7 / rich 14.0.0 / typer 0.16.0 / click 8.2.1, Python 3.13) with the full offline unit suite green (1249 passed) plus a CLI smoke test. The typer &lt;0.24 cap (click/semgrep clash) is unchanged. No functional change on Factory/TW (which ship newer). Lets the package install on Leap 16.0.</comment>
    <requestid>1363651</requestid>
  </revision>
  <revision rev="3" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>d4f90b35e6e8f991879bca010134c4a5</srcmd5>
    <version>2.5.1</version>
    <time>1785878930</time>
    <user>dimstar_suse</user>
    <comment>Update to version 2.5.1

Upstream's first tagged releases; the package moves from a pinned git snapshot
to the release tags.

Notable: JSON and SARIF reports now carry a canonical inspection ledger, so a
consumer can tell a clean zero-finding scan apart from one that did not execute
reliably. Also adds SKILLSPECTOR_MAX_LLM_CONCURRENCY for rate-limited providers,
exact-version reads from Python lockfiles for OSV, and an OMS signature fix.

The full offline suite runs: 1555 passed, 12 skipped. One new upstream test is
deselected -- it re-execs the CLI with PYTHONPATH replaced by the source tree,
so the child has no .dist-info and dies with PackageNotFoundError; it assumes an
editable dev install. Its assertion is covered in-process by
test_build_server_registers_scan_skill, which does run.</comment>
    <requestid>1369456</requestid>
  </revision>
  <revision rev="4" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>af02eb442bf39ef34453b0edcf34a91f</srcmd5>
    <version>2.9.6</version>
    <time>1787324183</time>
    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>Thu Aug 20 18:46:27 UTC 2026 - Martin Pluskal &lt;mpluskal@suse.com&gt;

- Update to version 2.9.6:
  * MCP registry posture scanning
  * Ollama, Azure OpenAI, and generic OpenAI-compatible providers
  * Opt-in discovery of an author-shipped baseline
    (.skillspector-baseline.yaml)
  * Detect insecure deserialization (AST10, TT6, DS1-DS4)
  * Detect whitespace-padding prompt injection (P9)
  * HIGH SC8 when a skill ships __pycache__ or .pyc
  * Bound URL, zip and git ingest to limit resource exhaustion
  * SC4 reports only verified OSV hits; use lockfile versions
  * HTTP MCP rejects local filesystem targets and local YARA dirs
  * Reject symlink traversal in skill content; disable git
    symlinks on clone
  * PE3 no longer flags ordinary OAuth &quot;access token&quot;
    documentation
  * LLM analyzers retry or isolate malformed responses and
    connection failures; incomplete analysis is recorded as
    skipped
  * Reduce false positives (instructional prose, Markdown
    tables, JS RegExp.exec, negated safety constraints, OMS
    signatures)
  * Map YARA matches via byte offsets; scope the locality guard
    to the built-in rule namespace
- Switch source to the official GitHub release tarball
- Drop _service, skillspector-2.5.1.obscpio and
  skillspector.obsinfo
- Add packaging &gt;= 24.0 (requirement parsing for OSV lookups)</comment>
    <requestid>1372636</requestid>
  </revision>
  <revision rev="5" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>801e8f075004632f757a83ef38425636</srcmd5>
    <version>2.10.0</version>
    <time>1787849592</time>
    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>Update skillspector to 2.10.0.

Upstream 2.10.0 (2026-08-26) adds bounded inspection of hidden and nested
ZIP/DOCX/XLSX/PPTX artifacts (HIGH SC9 for concealed executables), opt-in
transitive reference scanning, recognition of AISOP/AISP structured skill
bundles, EA5 detection for external model/provider selection,
SKILLSPECTOR_OUTPUT_LANGUAGE and risk_assessment.max_issue_severity, and it
floors an otherwise SAFE recommendation to CAUTION when requested LLM
analysis degrades.

Packaging notes:

- langgraph-cli dropped from BuildRequires and Requires. Upstream moved
  langgraph-cli[inmem] out of the base install into a new langgraph-dev
  extra, and neither the shipped code nor the test suite imports it.
  python-langgraph-cli remains in Factory for anyone who wants it.

- Four tests new in this release are deselected in %check because 2.10.0
  analyses artifacts more slowly than the ceilings those tests assume, and
  they fail on aarch64. Each was re-run undeselected in the build chroot on
  an otherwise idle machine first, so these are deterministic results rather
  than builder contention, and the spec records the per-test timings:

  * test_five_megabyte_normalized_static_scan_stays_below_memory_ceiling
    (5 MB artifact) takes 30.07 s, pinned exactly at the scanner's own
    MAX_STATIC_ANALYSIS_SECONDS_PER_ARTIFACT = 30.0 s budget
    (observed_seconds 30.047 vs limit_seconds 30.0), so the inspection
    ledger fail-closes to outcome=partial / reason_code=runtime_limit and
    the test's &quot;completed&quot; assertion cannot hold. The memory property the
    test exists for still holds: peak 2.1 MiB against its own 64 MiB
    ceiling.
  * test_rd04_large_file_pair_detects_start_boundary_and_end and
    test_nine_case_contract_across_public_surfaces (~1.26 MB fixture) take
    33 s and 34 s and hit the same budget.
  * test_dense_directory_discovery_and_cache_complete_with_modest_real_elapsed_time
    allows 5 s to walk and cache 256 one-byte files and takes 19.7 s, about
    77 ms per one-byte file; this release moved nested-artifact inspection,
    artifact classification and reference resolution into that path.

  This is the fail-closed resource bound behaving as designed, not a
  detection regression, and the same bounds stay covered by the tests that
  monkeypatch the budget instead of racing it. Worth reporting upstream.
  Note test_cross_window_separator_pair_across_public_surfaces was checked
  the same way, passes, and is deliberately NOT deselected.

The rest of the offline unit suite runs at build time; rpmlint is unchanged
from 2.9.6 (0 errors, 0 badness).</comment>
    <requestid>1373934</requestid>
  </revision>
